				To install official Google GenAI SDK and set my API key on Debian 13
				====================================================================
Yes, you can use Visual Studio Code directly without installing the SDK if your goal is only to chat with Gemini or get AI coding assistance.
If you want to write software or scripts that call the Gemini API, you must install the official SDK.


Option 2: Installing the Official SDK on Debian 13

If you are building an application in Python or JavaScript/TypeScript, follow these steps on Debian 13 (Trixie). Note that Debian enforces externally managed Python environments (PEP 668), so you must use a virtual environment.

1. Get Your API Key
Generate a Key. Visit Google AI Studio and click Create API key to generate your personal key. (AI assistants cannot provide or share their own API keys for security and billing reasons). 

2. Install the Python SDK (Recommended)
Create a Virtual Environment. Open your Debian terminal and set up an isolated environment:

	sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y python3-venv python3-pip
	mkdir my-gemini-app && cd my-gemini-app
	python3 -m venv .venv
	source .venv/bin/activate

Install the Package. Install the current official package (google-genai, which replaces the legacy google-generativeai library):

	pip install -U google-genai
	
3. Set Your API Key on Debian

	export GEMINI_API_KEY="YOUR_ACTUAL_API_KEY"
	
• Permanent (Across Reboots). Add the export line to your shell profile:

	echo 'export GEMINI_API_KEY="YOUR_ACTUAL_API_KEY"' >> ~/.bashrc
	source ~/.bashrc
	
4. Test Your Setup

from google import genai

# Automatically reads GEMINI_API_KEY from your environment
client = genai.Client()

response = client.models.generate_content(
    model="gemini-2.5-flash",
    contents="Hello from Debian 13!",
)
print(response.text)
